{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2590","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2590","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2590\",\"parent_revision\":\"cdf07efbafe897cdd0a627a1a39b250bd39e4e3e6d4d38429716806cf1649f40\",\"root_revision\":\"cdf07efbafe897cdd0a627a1a39b250bd39e4e3e6d4d38429716806cf1649f40\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: 2026-06-30\\n-->\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:25:51.412452Z</sub>\\n\\nVerified against primary source files (2026-06-09).\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Claim 1**\\n\\n- Claim: `GistTaskLayer._write_through` catches `ArtifactWriteError` and re-raises (no retry/backoff).\\n- Verdict: VERIFIED\\n- Evidence: Lines 622\u2013629 of `sam_schema/core/gist_task_layer.py` show `try: self._artifact_client.store(...) except ArtifactWriteError: _log.error(...) raise`. The except clause logs and immediately re-raises \u2014 no retry loop, no backoff, no silent suppression.\\n- Source: `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/gist_task_layer.py` lines 622\u2013629\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Claim 2**\\n\\n- Claim: `GitHubGistArtifactProvider.store_artifact_content` calls `gist.edit()` with no retry.\\n- Verdict: VERIFIED\\n- Evidence: Lines 576\u2013601 of `backlog_core/artifact_provider.py` show `store_artifact_content` calls `gist.edit(files={filename: InputFileContent(content)})` directly (line 601) with no surrounding retry loop, no backoff, and no exception handler. `GithubException` (403/429) propagates uncaught from this method.\\n- Source: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/artifact_provider.py` lines 576\u2013601\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Claim 3**\\n\\n- Claim: `test_gist_write_through.py` exists in `tests_sam/`.\\n- Verdict: VERIFIED\\n- Evidence: File exists at `plugins/development-harness/tests_sam/test_gist_write_through.py`. It imports from `sam_schema.core.gist_task_layer`, `sam_schema.core.artifact_registry_client`, and `sam_schema.core.exceptions`, and covers AC1, AC2, AC3, and AC7 using fakes with no live GitHub token required.\\n- Source: `plugins/development-harness/tests_sam/test_gist_write_through.py` lines 1\u201330\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Path correction (not a claim in the item, but a factual discrepancy)**\\n\\n- The item lists `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gist_task_layer.py` and `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/artifact_registry_client.py`. The actual files are at `sam_schema/core/gist_task_layer.py` and `sam_schema/core/artifact_registry_client.py`. The `backlog_core/artifact_provider.py` path is correct.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Files\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/gist_task_layer.py` \u2014 write-through flush path\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/artifact_provider.py` \u2014 `GitHubGistArtifactProvider.store()` which is called by `artifact_client.store()`\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- `sam_task state=complete` fails for the last task when many tasks have run in the same session\\n- Plan state is left inconsistent (task shows `in-progress` after completion)\\n- Orchestrator cannot proceed to `complete-implementation` because it can't confirm all tasks are COMPLETE\\n- Triggers a frustrating retry loop where every retry makes more API calls\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **fix: gisttasklayer triggers github api write on every sam_task state change \u2014 secondary rate limit risk** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: cast() removal session 2026-06-05\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-06-05\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-06-08)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-29T06:31:26.481782Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA SUMMARY (2026-06-29)\\n\\nGoal:\\n- Fix GistTaskLayer so that a GitHub secondary rate limit response (403/429) on Gist write-through does not propagate as an unhandled exception to the MCP caller, and local state changes are always committed regardless of remote write failure.\\n\\nSuccess Output:\\n- sam_task(action='state', status='complete') returns a successful MCP response even when the GitHub Gist API returns a 403 secondary rate limit.\\n- Local YAML file shows status: complete after the call.\\n- A WARNING log entry is emitted containing plan ID, issue number, and rate-limit signal.\\n- Genuine permission errors (token missing 'gist' scope) still propagate as ArtifactWriteError.\\n- All existing tests in tests_sam/test_gist_write_through.py pass unchanged.\\n- New tests for 403/429 path use deterministic in-memory fakes (rate-limit flag variant of _InMemoryArtifactStore).\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n\\nConditions and Verification:\\n\\n1. Rate-limit detection | AVAILABLE\\n   Basis: artifact_provider.py line 130 defines _GIST_FORBIDDEN_STATUS = 403.\\n   PyGithub GithubException has .status and .data[\\\"message\\\"]. The secondary\\n   rate-limit response body contains \\\"secondary rate limit\\\" or \\\"abuse detection\\\".\\n   The permission-denied 403 (missing 'gist' scope) has a distinct message\\n   (lines 744-746 of artifact_provider.py). Two 403 subtypes are distinguishable\\n   via exc.data[\\\"message\\\"] content inspection.\\n\\n2. Root cause (exception wrapping gap) | AVAILABLE\\n   CONFIRMED by code reading: GitHubGistArtifactProvider.store_artifact_content()\\n   (artifact_provider.py:573) does NOT wrap GithubException in BacklogError,\\n   unlike set_manifest() which does (line 508 wraps in BacklogError). The\\n   ArtifactRegistryClient.store() catch clause (line 174) catches\\n   (BacklogError, OSError, ValueError) \u2014 GithubException is NOT in this tuple.\\n   Result: GithubException(403) from gist.edit() escapes unhandled. This is\\n   the confirmed root cause.\\n\\n3. Fix placement | AVAILABLE\\n   Fix in two layers:\\n   (a) GitHubGistArtifactProvider.store_artifact_content(): wrap GithubException\\n       in BacklogError to match set_manifest() consistency. This ensures\\n       ArtifactRegistryClient.store() catches it and wraps it as ArtifactWriteError.\\n   (b) GistTaskLayer._write_through(): catch ArtifactWriteError, inspect for\\n       rate-limit signal in the reason string, degrade gracefully (log WARNING,\\n       return) for rate-limit; re-raise for genuine errors.\\n   create_plan() keeps its hard-fail behavior per ADR-2509-5.\\n\\n4. Retry vs degrade | EVIDENCE-DERIVED (mutation path only)\\n   Basis: 60s \u00d7 3 retry = 3+ minutes; MCP callers typically time out before\\n   that completes. Graceful degradation (log WARNING, return success, Gist stale\\n   until next mutation) is the correct MVP for the mutation path. create_plan\\n   retains hard-fail per ADR-2509-5.\\n   Validation step: check MCP server timeout in scripts/run_sam_server.py\\n   before deciding to implement retry instead of degrade.\\n\\n5. Test fake design | AVAILABLE\\n   _InMemoryArtifactStore in test_gist_write_through.py already has\\n   force_store_failure=True pattern (line 52-61). A new force_rate_limit=True\\n   flag raises a BacklogError with \\\"secondary rate limit\\\" in the message.\\n   Existing tests (test_mutation_raises_on_gist_write_failure, etc.) use\\n   force_store_failure=True and test genuine-error propagation \u2014 they pass\\n   unchanged because rate-limit degrade is a separate code path.\\n\\n6. create_plan behavior | AVAILABLE\\n   Scope: mutations degrade gracefully. create_plan retains hard-fail per\\n   ADR-2509-5 (gist_task_layer.py lines 18-19). Rate-limit on create_plan\\n   is a genuine failure \u2014 the plan is not durably stored.\\n\\nAssumptions to Confirm (EVIDENCE-DERIVED):\\n- MCP server timeout makes retry-in-mutations impractical | Validation: read\\n  scripts/run_sam_server.py before implementing retry to confirm timeout config.\\n\\nConcerns flagged for review:\\n- store_artifact_content() exception wrapping gap is the root cause \u2014 fix is\\n  to match set_manifest() pattern (wrap GithubException in BacklogError).\\n- Rate-limit distinguishability relies on message-text heuristic against\\n  GitHub API response bodies; recommend conservative default (only degrade on\\n  confirmed rate-limit pattern, propagate all other 403s).\\n</div>\\n\\n### Artifact Classification\\n\\n- Primary artifact type: Bug fix\\n- Behavioral boundary: The observable failure is `sam_task(action='state', status='complete')` returning `ArtifactWriteError` to the MCP caller when GitHub responds with a secondary rate limit 403. The fix is done when that MCP call succeeds and the local task state is updated correctly regardless of transient GitHub rate limits.\\n- Domain-semantic evaluation target: Acceptance criteria must prove (a) local YAML state is always updated on any mutation even when GitHub rejects the write, and (b) the MCP tool does not surface `ArtifactWriteError` to the caller on transient 403/429 responses from the GitHub Gist API.\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Open a repo with several SAM plans linked to GitHub issues and a `GITHUB_TOKEN` with `gist` scope.\\n2. Create or reuse a plan with 6+ tasks. Complete tasks T1\u2013T5 via `sam_task(action='state', status='complete')` in rapid succession (within a 10-minute window) to build up the GitHub API call count.\\n3. While completing tasks, also trigger `sam_task(action='claim')` and `sam_task(action='update')` calls to simulate realistic orchestrator activity (plan-index reads, field updates).\\n4. On the final task completion call (`sam_task(action='state', status='complete')`), observe the 403 response from the GitHub Gist API.\\n5. Observe the MCP tool call failing with `ArtifactWriteError`. The task remains in `in-progress` status in the local YAML file. The orchestrator cannot call `sam_plan(action='status')` and see all tasks COMPLETE.\\n\\n### Output / Evidence\\n\\n- MCP response error: `{\\\"error\\\": \\\"ArtifactWriteError\\\", \\\"plan_id\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"issue\\\": N}` or similar\\n- GitHub API response body: `\\\"API rate limit exceeded for user ID ... secondary rate limit\\\"` or `\\\"abuse detection mechanism\\\"`\\n- Local YAML shows task in `in-progress` after the failed `state=complete` call \u2014 `grep 'status: in-progress' ~/.dh/projects/.../plan/P*.yaml`\\n- Code path verified in source:\\n  - `gist_task_layer.py` lines 748-776 (`update_task_status`): calls `self._local.update_task_status()` then `self._write_through(plan_id, issue)` \u2014 write-through failure raises and the MCP call fails, leaving the local state inconsistent\\n  - `gist_task_layer.py` lines 568-592 (`_write_through`): bare `ArtifactWriteError` re-raise, no retry\\n  - `artifact_registry_client.py` lines 135-163 (`store`): bare `except (BacklogError, OSError, ValueError)` \u2014 `GithubException` from `store_artifact_content` is NOT in this catch list and propagates unwrapped through `set_manifest` path; in the `store_artifact_content` path `GithubException` also propagates unhandled\\n  - `artifact_provider.py` lines 565-590 (`store_artifact_content`): calls `gist.edit()` with no retry or backoff; `GithubException` propagates\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n7/10 \u2014 Reproducible in any real multi-task session with 6+ tasks completing in under 10 minutes. Blocks `complete-implementation` from succeeding when it hits the last task in a busy session. The workaround (wait 60s and retry the failing `sam_task` call) works but each retry triggers additional API calls, worsening the rate limit situation. Not P0 because data is not permanently lost \u2014 the local YAML write (step 1 of `update_task_status`) succeeds before the Gist write-through attempt, so only remote sync is lost.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- `sam_task(action='state', status='complete')` fails with `ArtifactWriteError` on the Nth task when many tasks complete in a short window\\n- Plan state is left inconsistent: task shows `in-progress` locally after a completion call that succeeded locally but failed remotely\\n- Orchestrator cannot proceed to `complete-implementation` because `sam_plan(action='status')` shows the task as not complete\\n- Retry loop: every retry adds more Gist API calls, compounding the rate limit pressure\\n- Affects any session that completes more than ~5-6 tasks within a 10-minute window \u2014 common for typical 6-10 task plans\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- `sam_task` mutations succeed even under GitHub secondary rate limits\\n- Local task state is always authoritative and always updated\\n- Orchestrator `complete-implementation` gate can proceed without manual intervention\\n- Removes a class of session-interrupting errors that require human attention to diagnose and recover from\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen `sam_task(action='state', status='complete')` is called and the GitHub Gist write returns a 403 or 429 secondary rate limit response:\\n\\n1. The local YAML task status is updated to `complete` (this already works \u2014 the bug is in step 2).\\n2. The Gist write-through is retried with exponential backoff (minimum viable fix), OR the error is caught and logged as a warning without propagating to the MCP caller (graceful degradation).\\n3. The MCP tool call returns a successful response reflecting the updated task status.\\n4. The task appears as `complete` in subsequent `sam_plan(action='status')` calls.\\n\\nThe design intent principle from `CLAUDE.md` applies: \\\"local state must always succeed; remote sync should be resilient to transient failures.\\\" The fix restores this intent.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\nAfter the fix, `_write_through()` (or its callers) should:\\n\\n- Detect secondary rate limit responses (HTTP 403/429 with GitHub secondary rate limit body)\\n- Either retry with exponential backoff (minimum viable), or catch the error and mark the remote sync as pending without failing the MCP call (graceful degradation)\\n- Log the rate limit event at WARNING level so it is visible in logs without surfacing to the caller\\n- Return from mutations successfully even when the remote Gist write is deferred or retried\\n\\nThe test suite at `tests_sam/test_gist_write_through.py` should be extended with deterministic fakes that simulate 403 rate limit responses to verify both the retry path and the graceful degradation path.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Given `sam_task(action='state', status='complete')` where the GitHub Gist API returns a 403 secondary rate limit response, the MCP tool call succeeds and returns the updated task status \u2014 not `ArtifactWriteError`.\\n2. Given the above scenario, the local YAML file shows `status: complete` for the task after the call \u2014 local state is never rolled back due to a remote write failure.\\n3. Given `sam_plan(action='status')` called after the above scenario, the task appears as `complete` in the status summary.\\n4. Given a 403 rate limit event, a WARNING log entry is emitted containing the plan ID, issue number, and the fact that the Gist write was rate-limited \u2014 so the event is observable without failing the call.\\n5. Given a transient 403 followed by a successful retry (if retry path is implemented), the Gist YAML is eventually consistent with the local YAML.\\n6. Given a non-rate-limit 403 (e.g., `gist` scope missing from token), the error still propagates as before \u2014 the fix must not suppress genuine permission errors.\\n7. The existing tests in `tests_sam/test_gist_write_through.py` continue to pass. New tests for the 403/429 path use deterministic in-memory fakes that simulate rate limit responses.\\n\\n### Questions for Human\\n\\n- Should the fix be minimum viable (retry+backoff in `_write_through` or `ArtifactRegistryClient.store`) or graceful degradation (local state always wins, remote sync is best-effort)? The design intent in `CLAUDE.md` supports graceful degradation, but retry+backoff is simpler to implement and test. Both are described in Fix Options in the issue body.\\n- Is there a maximum acceptable retry wait time? GitHub recommends waiting 60 s for secondary rate limits. A 3-retry exponential backoff could add up to ~4 minutes of wall time to a failing mutation. Graceful degradation avoids this latency entirely.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/gist_task_layer.py` \u2014 write-through flush path (`_write_through`, all mutation methods) |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/artifact_registry_client.py` \u2014 `ArtifactRegistryClient.store()` \u2014 the catch that misses `GithubException` |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/artifact_provider.py` \u2014 `GitHubGistArtifactProvider.store_artifact_content()` and `set_manifest()` \u2014 bare Gist API calls with no retry |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/tests_sam/test_gist_write_through.py` \u2014 existing test suite with in-memory fakes |\\n| Docs | `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 GistTaskLayer section \u2014 design intent: \\\"local state must always succeed; remote sync should be resilient to transient failures\\\" |\\n| Docs | `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/gist_task_layer.py` lines 1-45 \u2014 module docstring with ADR references (ADR-2509-5 write-path contract) |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None\\n- Blocks: None directly \u2014 but this bug blocks `complete-implementation` from running cleanly in any session with 6+ tasks, which is the common case for typical SAM plans\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n- None \u2014 root cause is verified in source, fix options are enumerated, test infrastructure exists\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall / Medium \u2014 The minimum viable fix (retry+backoff in `_write_through` or `ArtifactRegistryClient.store`) is Small: one targeted change, one new test. The graceful degradation fix (local state always wins, remote sync best-effort) is Medium: requires changing the error propagation contract across `_write_through` and all callers, plus new tests for the deferred-sync path. Decision on which approach determines final effort estimate.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:26:53.779160Z</sub>\\n\\n- Stated design intent (from CLAUDE.md): \\\"local state must always succeed; remote sync should be resilient to transient failures\\\"\\n- Alignment assessment: DIVERGENT\\n- Analysis: Current behavior diverges on the \\\"resilient to transient failures\\\" half. `_write_through` catches `ArtifactWriteError` and re-raises it unconditionally, propagating the failure to the `sam_task` MCP caller. A transient GitHub 403 or 429 on `gist.edit()` causes the entire `sam_task` state mutation to fail \u2014 the local write succeeds but the response returns an error, which is inconsistent with the stated intent that remote sync failures should not block local state. The fix (content-hash deduplication) addresses the rate-limit trigger but does not add resilience to transient remote failures on the writes that do proceed. A fuller alignment with the stated intent would also require best-effort write-through with local-authoritative fallback rather than hard failure on remote write errors.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- Every `sam_task` state-change mutation in `GistTaskLayer` routes through `_write_through`.\\n- `_write_through` \u2192 `ArtifactRegistryClient.store` \u2192 `GitHubGistArtifactProvider.store_artifact_content` \u2192 `gist.edit()` is the complete call chain for every write.\\n- The content-hash dedup shortcut (sidecar `.sha256` file) is the fix's primary mechanism for eliminating redundant `gist.edit()` calls.\\n- No other callers of `gist.edit()` exist in the mutation path outside `store_artifact_content`.\\n- The test file is the sole test suite for this call chain; no integration tests against live GitHub exist in-repo.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Groomed (2026-06-08)\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-08T08:13:02.209042Z</sub>\\n\\n### Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:25:18.292826Z</sub>\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:26:28.259331Z</sub>\\n\\n- Type: defect\\n- Rationale: The failure is traceable through a deterministic call chain \u2014 `gist.edit()` raises `GithubException` 403/429 \u2192 propagates through `store_artifact_content` \u2192 `ArtifactRegistryClient.store` raises `ArtifactWriteError` \u2192 `GistTaskLayer._write_through` re-raises \u2192 every `sam_task` state mutation fails. The root cause is a missing content-hash deduplication gate: the write-through fires unconditionally on every mutation even when the plan YAML has not changed. This is a behavioral defect in the write path, not a design ambiguity or recurring pattern requiring systemic change.\\n- Analysis Method: traceable-failure (call chain from `gist.edit()` through all layers identified)\\n- Scenario Target: Any workflow that calls `sam_task(action=\\\"state\\\", ...)` or `sam_task(action=\\\"update\\\", ...)` on a plan linked to a GitHub issue \u2014 all such calls trigger an unconditional `gist.edit()` PATCH, risking secondary rate limit (403) from GitHub.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n| File | Role | Change Surface |\\n|------|------|----------------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/gist_task_layer.py` | Producer \u2014 `_write_through` (lines 569\u2013639) | Error handling: catches `ArtifactWriteError` and re-raises; content-hash dedup added. Every mutation method calls this after local write. |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/artifact_registry_client.py` | Consumer/orchestrator \u2014 `ArtifactRegistryClient.store` (lines 117\u2013181) | Called by `_write_through`; performs manifest-entry registration then calls `provider.store_artifact_content`; raises `ArtifactWriteError` on `BacklogError`, `OSError`, or `ValueError`. |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/artifact_provider.py` | Producer \u2014 `GitHubGistArtifactProvider.store_artifact_content` (lines 576\u2013601) | Calls `gist.edit()` directly with no retry or backoff; raises `GithubException` on 403/429 which propagates as `BacklogError` to the client layer. |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/tests_sam/test_gist_write_through.py` | Test suite | Covers AC1/AC2/AC3/AC7; uses fakes, no live GitHub token required. |\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`sam_task(action='state', status='complete')` goes through `GistTaskLayer.update_task_status()` which calls `artifact_client.store()` (a Gist write) synchronously on every invocation. When multiple tasks complete in a short window \u2014 e.g. T1\u2013T6 completing across a 10-minute session, plus the orchestrator's own state checks and artifact registrations \u2014 the combined GitHub API call volume triggers GitHub's secondary rate limit (403 abuse detection).\\n\\nThe rate limit error message: \\\"API rate limit exceeded for user ID ... If you reach out to GitHub Support ... For more on scraping GitHub and how it may affect your rights...\\\"\\n</div>\\n\\n## Root Cause\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`GistTaskLayer` is a write-through cache: every mutation (claim, state update, field update, append_task, finalize) flushes the full plan YAML to a GitHub Gist. This is correct for durability, but:\\n\\n1. No retry with exponential backoff on 403/429 responses\\n2. No rate-limit-aware batching or debouncing\\n3. No graceful degradation \u2014 the error propagates up and fails the MCP tool call entirely, leaving the task state un-updated locally\\n</div>\\n\\n## Fix Options\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Add retry with exponential backoff on 403/429 in `artifact_client.store()` \u2014 minimum viable fix\\n2. Debounce: collect multiple mutations within a 1-second window and flush once \u2014 reduces write amplification\\n3. Graceful degradation: on 403/429, persist state locally and schedule a background sync \u2014 most resilient\\n\\nThe minimum viable fix is (1): catch `github.GithubException` with status 403/429 in the Gist write path, wait 60s, retry up to 3 times before surfacing the error.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"052f909446f466a9e6423db13dbd6a8632e787dfc33079820334c895d0069f2f\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCKz0A\"}"}